{"questions":[{"question_id":"nzFCxAyY4jwP8rrB","paperTitle":"AIEEE 2012","year":2012,"examDate":2012,"subject":"chemistry","chapter":"alcohols-phenols-and-ethers","chapterGroup":"organic-chemistry","topic":"properties-preparation-and-uses-of-phenols","type":"mcq","marks":4,"negMarks":1,"permalink":"orthonitrophenol-is-less-soluble-in-water-than-p-and-m-2012-marks-4-nzfcxayy4jwp8rrb.htm","content":"Ortho–Nitrophenol is less soluble in water than p– and m– Nitrophenols because :","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"o–Nitrophenol is more volatile in steam than those of m – and p–isomers"},{"identifier":"B","content":"o–Nitrophenol shows Intramolecular H–bonding"},{"identifier":"C","content":"o–Nitrophenol shows Intermolecular H–bonding"},{"identifier":"D","content":"Melting point of o–Nitrophenol is lower than those of m–and p–isomers."}],"correct_options":["B"],"explanation":"As in ortho-Nitrophenol H of -OH is already in Intra-moleculer H Bond and become stable so it will not perticipate in H bonding with H<sub>2</sub>O by breaking this H bonding. That is why solublity of Ortho–Nitrophenol is less in water.\n<img src=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/3xmhtm2xjkbg2hr5r/5e92dd64-faac-40da-b279-3a8c843abdb3/7fdeb4f0-aeca-11ea-9207-bfdf7589d371/file-3xmhtm2xjkbg2hr5s-320w.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;height: auto;display: block;margin: 0 auto;\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"AIEEE 2012 Chemistry - Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers Question 183 English Explanation 1\">\n<br><br>para– and meta– Nitrophenols both shows inter-moleculer H bonding with H<sub>2</sub>O. That is why both are more soluble in water.\n<picture><source media=\"(max-width: 320px)\" srcset=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/3xmhtm2xjkbg2ubhe/30b86fe1-1907-4792-923d-7771bf92af03/dd478f30-aecb-11ea-9207-bfdf7589d371/file-3xmhtm2xjkbg2ubhf-320w.jpg\"><source media=\"(max-width: 500px)\" srcset=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/3xmhtm2xjkbg2ubhe/30b86fe1-1907-4792-923d-7771bf92af03/dd478f30-aecb-11ea-9207-bfdf7589d371/file-3xmhtm2xjkbg2ubhf-500w.jpg\"><img src=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/3xmhtm2xjkbg2ubhe/30b86fe1-1907-4792-923d-7771bf92af03/dd478f30-aecb-11ea-9207-bfdf7589d371/file-3xmhtm2xjkbg2ubhf-680w.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;height: auto;display: block;margin: 0 auto;\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"AIEEE 2012 Chemistry - Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers Question 183 English Explanation 2\"></picture>\n<picture><source media=\"(max-width: 320px)\" srcset=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/3xmhtm2xjkbg2x3d6/ade8cfc4-dee4-4298-9649-eb9a29a7d3c8/2a6f9aa0-aecc-11ea-9207-bfdf7589d371/file-3xmhtm2xjkbg2x3d7-320w.jpg\"><source media=\"(max-width: 500px)\" srcset=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/3xmhtm2xjkbg2x3d6/ade8cfc4-dee4-4298-9649-eb9a29a7d3c8/2a6f9aa0-aecc-11ea-9207-bfdf7589d371/file-3xmhtm2xjkbg2x3d7-500w.jpg\"><img src=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/3xmhtm2xjkbg2x3d6/ade8cfc4-dee4-4298-9649-eb9a29a7d3c8/2a6f9aa0-aecc-11ea-9207-bfdf7589d371/file-3xmhtm2xjkbg2x3d7-680w.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;height: auto;display: block;margin: 0 auto;\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"AIEEE 2012 Chemistry - Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers Question 183 English Explanation 3\"></picture>"}]}